\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{AI-Generated Text Detection}
\label{sec:related-detection}

Methods for distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text range from statistical tests \cite{gltr,grover,release_strategies} through zero-shot approaches \cite{detectgpt,fast_detectgpt,binoculars,dna_gpt} to learned classifiers \cite{ghostbuster,feat_aug_transformer,reliable_detection,ld_score}, with extensions to intrinsic dimensionality \cite{intrinsic_dimension}, surprisal diversity \cite{diveye}, and distributional gaps \cite{generator_barrier}; see \citet{wu_survey_2025} and \citet{jawahar_survey} for surveys.
Recent work moves beyond binary decisions---recognizing collaboration roles \cite{beyond_binary}, joint detection-attribution \cite{two_birds}, creator-editor modes \cite{beyond_final_actor}, multi-generator attribution \cite{semeval2024_task8}---but all target \emph{source} or \emph{production mode} taxonomies rather than recursive generation \emph{depth}; detection scores exhibit high cross-generator variance as depth predictors (binary F1 0.52--0.71; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}) \cite{paraphrase_evades,detection_possibilities}.
Our contribution is the task formulation (generation depth classification \cite{gutierrez_ordinal_survey,coral_ordinal}) and the finding that combining drifting and stationary features yields consistent cross-generator performance, orthogonal to source attribution \cite{uchendu_attribution,munir_looking_glass}.

\subsection{Model Collapse and Iterative Generation}
\label{sec:related-collapse}

Self-consuming generative models progressively lose distributional coverage \cite{self_consuming_mad,curse_of_recursion}, though data mixing mitigates collapse \cite{model_collapse_avoidable,strong_model_collapse}; these studies address \emph{training-loop} degradation, whereas we study \emph{inference chains} with fixed parameters.
Iterative self-refinement \cite{self_refine} targets output improvement through feedback rather than unconstrained recursive generation.
Two concurrent works are most directly relevant: \citet{broken_telephone} analyze paraphrasing and translation chains showing progressive information loss, and \citet{markovian_chains} demonstrate attractor dynamics bounding distinguishable depth to approximately three classes.
We differ by studying \emph{non-paraphrastic continuation} chains, framing depth as \emph{ordinal classification} (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+), and providing DDR feature-level diagnostics revealing \emph{what} converges (location) versus what remains stable (shape)---complementing their analysis of \emph{why} convergence occurs.
That all three works independently converge on ${\sim}$3 distinguishable classes---from Markovian dynamics \cite{markovian_chains}, information loss \cite{broken_telephone}, and feature compression (this work)---suggests a shared ceiling.

\subsection{Vocabulary Diversity and Stylometry}
\label{sec:related-diversity}

Declining lexical diversity in LLM text is documented in training \cite{curious_decline} and deployment \cite{writing_diversity}; our surface features (TTR, hapax ratio) are classical stylometric measures \cite{stamatatos_survey,koppel_authorship} that we extend beyond generator identity \cite{uchendu_attribution} to the depth dimension under top-$p$ sampling \cite{nucleus_sampling}.

\subsection{Data Quality and Provenance}
\label{sec:related-provenance}

Scalable data curation relies on quality signals \cite{dsir,qurating,ccnet}; generation depth is a complementary provenance signal.
Proactive watermarking \cite{watermark_llm,synthid_text,aaronson_watermark} embeds identifiable patterns, but strength decays across multi-step chains \cite{chainwash}, motivating passive methods; our NLL analysis connects to \citet{min_k_percent} (probability-tail membership inference), extending to full distributional analysis for depth.

This appendix presents feature ablation results supporting the complementarity analysis in \S\ref{sec:results-differential}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature set} & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{Top feature (imp.)} \\
\midrule
Full (7 features)     & 0.773 & hapax\_ratio (0.35) \\
Surface only (2)      & 0.688 & hapax\_ratio (0.41) \\
Reference only (5)    & 0.713 & entropy\_mean (0.27) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Feature ablation (8-gen, 10-fold stratified CV, 3-class). Full $>$ surface (+8.5\,pp) and Full $>$ reference (+6.0\,pp) confirms complementarity.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\end{table}

To assess whether depth signal extends beyond handcrafted features, we fine-tune a pretrained language model end-to-end as a depth classifier using QLoRA \cite{qlora}.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune Qwen3-0.6B-Base with LoRA rank 16, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 epochs, framing depth estimation as 5-class sequence classification (gen-0 through gen-4).\footnote{Evaluated on the original 6-generator subset; XGBoost 3-class F1\,=\,0.789. We expect 8-generator training to further improve given additional diversity; the comparison direction is preserved.}
For 3-class evaluation, we collapse predictions into gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ post hoc.
All results use the same LOGO protocol as the main experiments.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{QLoRA end-to-end depth classification vs.\ XGBoost on handcrafted features, under LOGO evaluation.}
\label{tab:qlora}
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{5-class F1} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Per-class F1 (5-class)} \\
\midrule
XGBoost (7 features) & 0.501 $\pm$ 0.038 & 0.789 & --- \\
QLoRA (Qwen3-0.6B)   & 0.583 $\pm$ 0.010 & 0.905 $\pm$ 0.003 & 0.999\,/\,0.796\,/\,0.413\,/\,0.259\,/\,0.447 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Results.}
QLoRA achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.003), a +11.6\,pp improvement over the XGBoost baseline (0.789).
On 5-class evaluation, QLoRA reaches F1\,=\,0.583 ($\pm$0.010) vs.\ 0.501 for XGBoost (+8.2\,pp), with the largest gains at gen-1 (0.796 vs.\ 0.634) and gen-2 (0.413 vs.\ 0.268).
The gen-2/gen-3 boundary remains the primary failure mode (gen-3 F1\,=\,0.259), consistent with the feature-level analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter robustness.}
A second run with LoRA rank 32 and seed 123 yields 5-class F1\,=\,0.587 ($\pm$0.005) and 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.004), confirming stability across hyperparameter and seed variation ($\Delta < 1$\,pp on both metrics).

\paragraph{Implications.}
The substantial 3-class improvement demonstrates that raw token embeddings contain depth signal beyond what summary statistics capture; the seven handcrafted features are a strong but lossy compression of the full distributional information.
End-to-end fine-tuning exploits token-level patterns (e.g., positional regularities, n-gram repetitions) that aggregate features cannot represent.
This confirms that the depth signal documented in our main analysis is real and exploitable beyond engineered features, while the handcrafted approach retains practical advantages: interpretability, computational efficiency (no GPU inference), and the ability to reveal the differential depth response pattern that motivates the task formulation.

Figure~\ref{fig:cross-temp} shows cross-temperature transfer results for the 6-generator LOGO pipeline.
We evaluate four train$\rightarrow$test temperature conditions using Qwen3-1.7B data regenerated at $t{=}1.0$ (remaining five generators retain $t{=}0.7$).

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_cross_temp.pdf}
\caption{Cross-temperature transfer evaluation (6-gen LOGO). (a) Overall F1 by train$\rightarrow$test temperature condition. Matched conditions (0.7$\rightarrow$0.7, 1.0$\rightarrow$1.0) outperform cross-temperature transfer, with 0.7$\rightarrow$1.0 showing the largest degradation ($-6.6$\,pp in 3-class F1). (b) Per-generator 3-class F1 heatmap: Qwen3-1.7B shows dramatic degradation under cross-temperature conditions (0.418) while other generators remain stable, indicating the temperature mismatch disproportionately affects the generator whose data was regenerated.}
\label{fig:cross-temp}
\end{figure*}

Key observations from the cross-temperature analysis:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
\item \textbf{Matched-temperature robustness}: Both $t{=}0.7$ and $t{=}1.0$ matched conditions yield comparable performance (3-class F1: 0.789 vs.\ 0.726), with the gap attributable to weaker depth signal at high temperature rather than classifier degradation.

\item \textbf{Asymmetric transfer}: Training at $t{=}1.0$ and testing at $t{=}0.7$ (F1\,=\,0.791) transfers well, but the reverse (0.7$\rightarrow$1.0, F1\,=\,0.723) degrades. This asymmetry reflects the information structure: features learned from high-variance (high-temperature) data generalize to low-variance settings, but not vice versa.

\item \textbf{Generator-specific vulnerability}: Qwen3-1.7B, the only generator with temperature-mismatched test data, drops to F1\,=\,0.418 under cross-temperature conditions while all other generators maintain F1 $>$ 0.76. This confirms that depth features are calibrated to the specific decoding regime.

\item \textbf{Binary robustness}: Binary classification (gen-0/1 vs.\ gen-2+) is more robust to temperature mismatch (0.900--0.958 across all conditions) than 3-class, suggesting the coarser gen-0 vs.\ machine boundary is less sensitive to decoding parameters than fine-grained depth discrimination.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Ethics Statement}
\label{sec:ethics}

All texts analyzed in this work are LLM-generated from publicly available Wikipedia seeds; no personal or sensitive data is involved.
Our method estimates the generation depth of a text passage (how many recursive generation steps it has undergone), not the identity of its author.
Individual authorship attribution is outside both the scope and the capability of this approach; privacy implications are minimal.
We acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be used to selectively suppress machine-generated text in contexts where such content is legitimate (e.g., accessibility tools, assistive writing).
We encourage deployment in data governance pipelines where provenance transparency, rather than content removal, is the objective.

\paragraph{Licenses.}
All generator models are publicly available under permissive licenses (Apache 2.0 for Pythia, OLMo, and Mistral; Qwen License for Qwen3; Llama Community License for Llama 3.2).
Wikipedia seed passages are distributed under CC BY-SA 3.0.

\paragraph{Compute budget.}
All experiments were conducted on 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32\,GB each), totaling approximately 62 GPU-hours.

\paragraph{Software.}
Experiments used Python 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.10.0 (CUDA 12.8), Transformers 4.57.6, XGBoost 3.2.0, scikit-learn 1.8.0, NumPy 2.2.6, pandas 3.0.2, and SciPy 1.17.1.

We evaluate depth fingerprinting under three prompt types using Pythia-2.8B: \emph{continuation} (``Continue the following text''), \emph{elaboration} (``Elaborate on the following text''), and \emph{inspired rewriting} (``Write a new passage inspired by the following text''). Each uses 995 seeds $\times$ 5 depths.

\paragraph{Within-prompt-type classification.}
5-fold CV macro-F1: continuation 0.78, rewriting 0.74, elaboration 0.73. All three prompt types exhibit the same differential depth response pattern: drifting features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show consistent monotonic trends, while stationary features (entropy std, tail decay) change by less than 5\%.

\paragraph{Cross-prompt-type transfer (LOPTO).}
Leave-one-prompt-type-out evaluation yields average F1\,=\,0.753, confirming that depth signal transfers across prompt types. Feature decay rates differ: continuation shows the strongest drift (16.9\% per depth step), followed by rewriting (6.1\%) and elaboration (1.4\%), consistent with continuation chains applying less semantic constraint on the output.

